{
  "id": "service_no_endpoints",
  "type": "troubleshooting",
  "question": "What does the selector field in ServiceSpec define, and what schema does the Endpoints object use to describe the IP addresses and ports of its backend entries?",
  "resource": "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Service",
  "scope": "Namespaced",
  "multi_hop": true,
  "ground_truth": {
    "answer": "The selector field in ServiceSpec is a map of string key-value pairs that identifies which Pods a Service targets. When selector is non-empty, Kubernetes maintains a corresponding Endpoints object (named identically to the Service) whose content is derived from that selector at runtime by the Endpoint controller. The Endpoints schema uses a subsets field containing a list of EndpointSubset objects. Each EndpointSubset has an addresses field (a list of EndpointAddress entries, one per Ready Pod whose labels match all selector key-value pairs) and a ports field (a list of EndpointPort entries mirroring the Service port definitions). If no Pods satisfy the selector — due to label mismatch or no Pods being in the Ready state — the subsets list is empty.",
    "context": [
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.ServiceSpec: selector is a string map; Kubernetes creates an Endpoints object matching Pods whose labels satisfy all selector entries",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Endpoints: automatically managed by Kubernetes; contains subsets of type EndpointSubset listing matching Ready Pod IPs and ports",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.EndpointSubset: addresses contains EndpointAddress entries (IP of Ready Pods); ports contains EndpointPort entries"
    ],
